## What Happened

.NET 11 removed the built-in DSA implementation on macOS. `DSA.Create()` now throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` because Apple's cryptographic frameworks (Security.framework / CryptoKit) do not support DSA, and .NET no longer ships an OpenSSL-based fallback on macOS.

DSA was marked obsolete (`SYSLIB0028`) since .NET 7, signaling this removal.

## How to Fix

Since you only need to **verify** existing signatures, use **BouncyCastle** (a pure managed crypto library that works on all platforms):

```csharp
// Install: dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography

using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public static bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] message, byte[] signature, DsaPublicKeyParameters publicKey)
{
    var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
    signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
    signer.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return signer.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

## Long-term recommendation

Migrate to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519** — both are supported natively across all .NET platforms and are cryptographically stronger than DSA.
